Below is a compact, self‑contained Java program that demonstrates the most common collection types (`List`, `Set`, `Map`) and a few handy operations (adding, sorting, iterating, filtering with streams, etc.).  
All the code is in one class with a `main` method so you can copy‑paste it into a file named `CollectionsDemo.java` and run it.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class CollectionsDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        /* ---------- List example (ArrayList) ---------- */
        List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();
        numbers.add(5);
        numbers.add(2);
        numbers.add(9);
        numbers.add(1);
        numbers.add(5);           // duplicate value

        System.out.println("Unsorted list: " + numbers);

        // Sort the list
        Collections.sort(numbers);
        System.out.println("Sorted list:   " + numbers);

        // Iterate with for‑each
        System.out.print("Numbers: ");
        for (int n : numbers) {
            System.out.print(n + " ");
        }
        System.out.println("\n");

        /* ---------- Set example (HashSet) ---------- */
        Set<String> uniqueNames = new HashSet<>();
        uniqueNames.add("Alice");
        uniqueNames.add("Bob");
        uniqueNames.add("Alice");   // duplicate ignored

        System.out.println("Unique names set: " + uniqueNames + "\n");

        /* ---------- LinkedList example ---------- */
        LinkedList<String> tasks = new LinkedList<>();
        tasks.add("Write report");
        tasks.addFirst("Prepare slides");
        tasks.addLast("Attend meeting");

        System.out.println("Tasks list: " + tasks + "\n");

        /* ---------- Map example (HashMap) ---------- */
        Map<String, Integer> ageMap = new HashMap<>();
        ageMap.put("Alice", 30);
        ageMap.put("Bob", 25);
        ageMap.put("Charlie", 35);

        // Retrieve a single value
        System.out.println("Alice's age: " + ageMap.get("Alice") + "\n");

        // Iterate over key/value pairs
        System.out.println("All age entries:");
        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : ageMap.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println(entry.getKey() + " -> " + entry.getValue());
        }
        System.out.println();

        /* ---------- Iterator example ---------- */
        Iterator<Integer> it = numbers.iterator();
        System.out.print("Numbers via iterator: ");
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            System.out.print(it.next() + " ");
        }
        System.out.println("\n");

        /* ---------- Stream filter example ---------- */
        List<Integer> evens = numbers.stream()
                                     .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
                                     .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println("Even numbers: " + evens);
    }
}
```

### What the program shows

| Feature | Code snippet | What it does |
|---------|--------------|--------------|
| **`List`** (`ArrayList`) | `List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();` | Ordered collection that allows duplicates. |
| **Sorting** | `Collections.sort(numbers);` | Sorts the list in natural order. |
| **Iteration** | `for (int n : numbers)` or `Iterator` | Two ways to traverse a collection. |
| **`Set`** (`HashSet`) | `Set<String> uniqueNames = new HashSet<>();` | Collection that automatically removes duplicates. |
| **`LinkedList`** | `LinkedList<String> tasks = new LinkedList<>();` | List that offers fast insertions/removals at both ends. |
| **`Map`** (`HashMap`) | `Map<String, Integer> ageMap = new HashMap<>();` | Key/value mapping. |
| **Retrieval** | `ageMap.get("Alice")` | Get value by key. |
| **Iteration over map** | `for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : ageMap.entrySet())` | Loop through all key/value pairs. |
| **Streams** | `numbers.stream().filter(n -> n % 2 == 0).collect(...)` | Functional style filtering/collecting. |

Feel free to modify the example to explore other collection implementations (`TreeSet`, `TreeMap`, `LinkedHashMap`, etc.) or to experiment with Java 8+ features like `forEach` and `Collectors`.
